{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 23, :comments ["<b>The sons of Issachar, etc.</b> Since it has preceded us that Issachar is a man of Torah, the Torah hinted in this name of Issachar that only this — Torah — is there as reward (sachar), and everything outside it — what advantage does a man have in all his toil? This is what they said in Berakhot in the chapter Tefillat haShachar (Berakhot 28b), that one must bless upon taking leave of the beit midrash: ״I give thanks before You, etc. I toil and they toil; I toil and receive reward (sachar) and they toil and do not receive reward,״ etc., thus far. And the reason is that an illusory acquisition cannot endure, and even though what stands is but vapor. Further, it hints that everything that exists in the world is reward (sachar) for it, for everything that HaShem created, He created only for its sake, as they of blessed memory expounded (Bereshit Rabbah 1:1) in the passage of Bereshit, etc. — for the sake of Torah, etc., He created heavens and earth. Further, it hints to what they of blessed memory said (Uktzin 3:12): the Holy One, blessed be He, is destined to bequeath to every righteous person three hundred and ten worlds, and this is its saying יֵשׁ שָׂכָר (yesh sachar)." "And its saying <b>of Tola, etc.</b>, the verse hinted at the conditions for acquiring Torah through the names of Issachar's families, and they are forty-eight, as they said (Avot 6:5) in the Mishnah that the Torah is acquired through forty-eight things. Now the meaning of Tola indicates two particulars: first, one should make oneself like this worm, as they of blessed memory said (Mo'ed Katan 16b) concerning David, who was called ״עֲדִינוֹ הָעֶצְנִי״ (2 Samuel 23:8). Second, that one should engage with his mouth like this worm (tola), whose strength is in its mouth, for man was created for the toil of the mouth. And these two things encompass all the branches of humility and all the branches of study that are enumerated among the forty-eight traits mentioned there." "And its saying <b>of Puvah, the Punite family</b> — there is reason to note why it added a letter that is not counted among the letters of the Name, for it should have said the family of the Puvi? Rather, it hinted to the remaining traits enumerated there among the forty-eight (Avot 6:5), which are minimizing speech, minimizing laughter, and the other branches similar to these. And this is its saying <b>of Puvah</b>, the Punite family — meaning, ״Puvah״ is an expression of mouth (peh), and it added to it the letter vav, which is the sod of the Tree of Life, which one engages in beyond all other mouths. Also, it hinted in the adjacency of the letter vav to the letter heh to indicate the sod of the union of the attributes through the engagement of Torah. And its saying <b>the Punite family</b>, meaning, that one must clear it of superfluities that are felt — even from laughter and from idle matters, for they prevent the fulfillment of Torah in a person. And I found that the pious of Israel wrote (Alshikh on Exodus 28:31) that the mouth of those who study Torah has the status of a service vessel in which the sacred service is performed, for there is no holiness like the holiness of Torah; therefore it is forbidden to speak with it even mundane words, even if they contain nothing forbidden. And this is what it hinted in the phrase <b>of Puvah</b>, which is a mouth toiling in Torah, in which dwells half of His name, blessed be He — <b>the Punite family</b>, meaning, one must clear from his mouth everything except uttering Torah; and in this is included one portion of the forty-eight traits, which are minimizing speech, minimizing laughter, minimizing pleasure, and similar to them in the branch of superfluities." "And its saying <b>of Yashuv</b> — it hints that one must increase in sitting (yeshivah) and not in a casual manner; also, one must settle (lehityasheshev) on every particular of the Torah until one stands on its essence, as is necessary in the depth of halakhah, for that is the essence of Torah. And in this is included one portion of the forty-eight traits, which are: in steady settling (yishuv), and settling one's heart in one's study, asking and answering, one who aims his tradition accurately, etc.; also what they enumerated there — listening with the ear, arranging the lips, understanding of the heart, etc., and similar to this in the branch of grasping the intelligibilia." "And its saying <b>of Shimron, the Shimronite family</b> — it hints to the additional vigilance (shemirah) that a man of Torah is obligated to practice beyond the general public, as they of blessed memory said (Yoma 86a): What constitutes desecration of the Name? Rav said: such as myself — I take meat and do not pay immediately, thus far. From this one may infer how much a man of Torah must guard himself in every particular of the commandments. And they of blessed memory said (Chovot haLevavot, Sha'ar haTeshuvah, ch. 5) that the early pious ones would guard fifty gates of permission lest they stumble in a gate of prohibition, and this is its saying ״of Shimron, the Shimronite family.״" "And the verse's saying <b>these are the families of Issachar by their countings</b> — the meaning of \"their countings\" is: a counting for good. And what is this counting? The verse explained and said: sixty-four thousand and three hundred. Here the master alluded to two matters: the first, that in this world they become a chariot for the Shekhinah, and this is what he alluded to in the statement \"four and sixty,\" which is one aspect of a single point — behold, ס״ה, which is the numerical value of אדנ״י. And he said it in this order to say that through the mediation of the scholar, the aspect of din, whose count is ס״ד, is refined, and the Shekhinah is rectified in her number, ס״ה, as they said, of blessed memory, in the Zohar (part II, 9a) on the verse ״וְהָאֵם רֹבֶצֶת עַל הָאֶפְרֹחִים״ etc. (Deuteronomy 22:6). And the second matter he grasped is for the World to Come, alluded to in His name — three hundred and ten worlds, and this is what he alluded to in the statement \"a thousand and three hundred.\"" "And before we stand on the intention, I will preface with the statement of Rabbi Yehoshua ben Levi (Uktzin 3:12) who said: the Holy One, blessed be He, is destined to bequeath, etc., three hundred and ten worlds, and he proves it from the verse's saying (Proverbs 8:21) ״לְהַנְחִיל אֹהֲבַי יֵשׁ״. It is difficult according to his words, of blessed memory: if the verse intends a number, why did the verse precede the number ten to the number of hundreds — for it should have said ״לְהַנְחִיל אֹהֲבַי שַׁ״י״ (310)? And I heard from the early ones a sweet reason: for this number alludes to four worlds, as is known — Atzilut, Beriah, Yetzirah, Asiyah — and each one includes ten. And the statement of Rabbi Yehoshua ben Levi comes to teach that the righteous will delight in an abundance of peace in the supernal world in four worlds, but their grasp in all of them is not equal; rather, in three of them they will grasp all the gradations that exist in them, and they are in the number three hundred. But in the supreme world, which is the world of Atzilut, not every righteous person can grasp more than a portion of the ten within it, which is itself also ten in its particulars — as mentioned, that each one includes ten. And you should know that the ten grasped from the supreme world are great and exalted far above all the three hundred, and therefore the verse preceded the ten to the hundreds and said ״לְהַנְחִיל״ etc. ״יֵ״שׁ״. And according to these things, in saying <b>a thousand</b> he intends the small portion alluded to in the letter yod, and assigned to it the number thousand, as they said, of blessed memory, that every ten includes ten and ten includes ten — so a single point can multiply to a thousand. And his saying \"and three hundred\" is according to its plain meaning, and this is what is alluded to in the name of Issachar — ״יֵשׁ שָׂכָר״ (\"there is reward\")."], :book :numbers, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 26, :notes {5 "The verse numbers the tribe of Issachar at 64,300. The commentator reads 64 (=ס״ד) as the aspect of din and adds the central point (=1, forming ס״ה=65, the numerical value of אדנ״י) for the Shekhinah's rectification; the remaining 300 allude to the 310 worlds of the World to Come. The statement 'a thousand and three hundred' (אֶלֶף וּשְׁלֹשׁ מֵאוֹת) is read as 'one thousand' (i.e., a single point multiplied) and 'three hundred.'"}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:15:26.769259880Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}